Demo 3 (PSI + Yao) live-LLM cross-provider replication
======================================================

Backend:    Anthropic Claude Haiku 4.5 via OpenRouter
Endpoint:   https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions
Auth:       OPENROUTER_API_KEY (Bearer)
Routed:     anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5  (Amazon Bedrock provider)
Model id (server-side): anthropic/claude-4.5-haiku-20251001
Temperature: 0.0
Max-turns:  16 (default)
N per cell: 20
Date:       2026-05-02

Why OpenRouter
--------------

Direct api.anthropic.com calls returned `invalid_request_error: credit
balance too low` for both API keys we had access to, despite the
console showing $15 USD remaining. We worked around this by routing
through OpenRouter, whose OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions endpoint
is wire-compatible with the existing OpenAIBackend (with the addition
of a `with_endpoint()` setter and a `from_env_var()` constructor for
the OPENROUTER_API_KEY env var).

Reproduce
---------

    cd ~/Code/cbcl-rs
    export OPENROUTER_API_KEY=...
    for cell in free disciplined cooperative native-cooperative native-attacker; do
        cargo run --release -p cbcl-arena --example glm_psi -- \
            --backend haiku --cell $cell --n 20
    done
    for cell in free disciplined cooperative native-cooperative native-attacker; do
        cargo run --release -p cbcl-arena --example glm_yao -- \
            --backend haiku --cell $cell --n 20
    done

Wall-time totals
----------------

PSI total wall-time (5 cells × N=20):    ~25 min
Yao total wall-time (5 cells × N=20):    ~25 min
Combined:                                ~50 min

Notes
-----

- OpenRouter cost for Demo 3 PSI+Yao: ~$0.30 USD at Haiku list pricing
  ($1/M input, $5/M output). Demo 1 (separate cbcl-mcp run, 14
  scenarios x N=100) added $0.66 USD. Total cross-provider Tier-3
  Haiku replication cost: ~$1.00 USD.
- OpenRouter throughput is comparable to Codex for this workload (no
  observed throttling).
- Model id reported in the OpenRouter response was
  `anthropic/claude-4.5-haiku-20251001` even though we sent
  `anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5`. OpenRouter normalises model ids on
  the response side; the routed model is the Anthropic Haiku 4.5 we
  asked for.
